Under Directive 2011/70/Euratom, a Member State's national programme for spent fuel and radioactive waste must include all of the following:

- The overall objectives of the Member State’s national policy in respect of spent fuel and radioactive waste management;
- The significant milestones and clear timeframes for the achievement of those milestones in light of the over-arching objectives of the national programme;
- An inventory of all spent fuel and radioactive waste and estimates for future quantities, including those from decommissioning, clearly indicating the location and amount of the radioactive waste and spent fuel in accordance with appropriate classification of the radioactive waste;
- The concepts or plans and technical solutions for spent fuel and radioactive waste management from generation to disposal;
- The concepts or plans for the post-closure period of a disposal facility’s lifetime, including the period during which appropriate controls are retained and the means to be employed to preserve knowledge of that facility in the longer term;
- The research, development and demonstration activities that are needed in order to implement solutions for the management of spent fuel and radioactive waste;
- The responsibility for the implementation of the national programme and the key performance indicators to monitor progress towards implementation;
- An assessment of the national programme costs and the underlying basis and hypotheses for that assessment, which must include a profile over time;
- The financing scheme(s) in force;
- A transparency policy or process as referred to in Article 10;
- If any, the agreement(s) concluded with a Member State or a third country on management of spent fuel or radioactive waste, including on the use of disposal facilities.

Additionally, the national programme together with the national policy may be contained in a single document or in a number of documents [[S2]].

ONR's guidance states that Licence Condition 23 (LC23) requires the licensee to produce an adequate safety case that identifies the limits and conditions necessary to keep the facility within a safe operating envelope in respect of any operation that may affect safety. These limits and conditions constitute the operating rules (ORs). The licensee should ensure ongoing compliance with its ORs and take appropriate action if any are exceeded, with incidents recorded, investigated, and reported under LC7. The requirements of LC23 apply to all limits and conditions in the safety case, not only those formally labelled as "operating rules" [[S1]][[S4]].

The guidance further states that when assessing OR identification or implementation, inspectors should confirm the licensee’s ORs adequately cover:

- all the permitted operating modes;
- all identified sources of hazard;
- all identified fault sequences;
- all levels of the defence in depth hierarchy;
- all claimed plant and equipment, operating ranges and the resources and support services required; and
- all human-related claims, including specific Suitably Qualified and Experienced Persons (SQEP) requirements and staffing levels [[S5]].

Additionally, the Safety Assessment Principles (SAPs) set ONR’s expectations that safety cases should identify operating limits and conditions and show how they will be implemented effectively. ORs should be usable, measurable, and clearly linked to safety case assumptions. They should cover safety settings, configuration and availability requirements, and the safe operating envelope, informed by Design Basis Analysis (DBA), Probabilistic Safety Analysis (PSA), and Severe Accident Analysis (SAA) where appropriate [[S8]].

In summary, ONR's guidance expects licensees to set limits and conditions for nuclear safety that comprehensively cover operational modes, hazards, fault sequences, defence in depth, equipment, and human factors, and to ensure these are clearly defined and linked to the safety case [[S1]][[S5]][[S8]].
